# Jobs

- `jobs.Runner.Start(ctx, kind, Subject{Type, ID}, createdBy, fn, opts...)` (options `jobs.Restricted(bool)`, `jobs.InPool(name)`) persists a queued job, creates `logs/jobs/<id>.log` (0600) and runs `fn(ctx, log)` on a bounded worker pool: the default pool (`Options.Workers`) or a named one (`Options.Pools`; build jobs use `build.JobPool`, so waiting builds never delay server or project jobs). At most one job per subject is active (`ErrSubjectBusy`). Workers are handed out per pool under `Runner.mu` in start order (`pool.queue`, `admitLocked`), never by goroutines racing for a slot; a job cancelled while queued, or at the moment it is admitted, ends `cancelled` without starting (no `started_at`).
- `fn` must honour `ctx` (cancelled by `Cancel` and by shutdown) and write human-readable progress and full error output to `log`. `log` is capped (`Options.MaxLogBytes`, default 16 MiB): beyond it one truncation marker is written and the rest discarded without blocking or failing the job; the runner's own final lines bypass the cap. The `detail` shown to clients is empty on success, the message of a `jobs.Fail(msg, cause)` error, or a generic message; panics are recovered into `error`.
- Status transitions are persisted (`queued`, `running`, then `success`, `error` or `cancelled`); final writes use a background context so they survive shutdown.
- `Done(id)` closes when the job leaves this process; the log follow endpoint uses it to end streams.
- Access: `restricted` jobs (set by `httpapi.startJob` for admin routes) expose status and detail to developers but only admin sessions may read their log or cancel them; other logs are readable by any developer identity and cancellable by the creator or an admin (`httpapi.authorizeJob`). `?follow=1` streams are capped per account.
- `Job.created_by_username` is read with the job in one `LEFT JOIN accounts` query (`store.jobCols`/`jobFrom`; `INSERT ... RETURNING` uses a scalar subquery); it is null whenever `created_by` is. Never resolve creators per row.
- Job kinds and subject types are listed in `internal/apitypes/jobs.go`; add new ones there. `project.build` uses the project subject (serialised with clone/pull/branch); a job cancelled while queued never runs its body, so anything that must be recorded for it is done from `Done` (see `build.Service.Watch`).
- Certificate issue and renew use the same restricted `certificate/acme` subject. The certificate coordinator checks reservations before synchronous mode or settings writes and waits for certificate work before the store closes.
- Verify with `go test ./internal/jobs/... ./internal/httpapi/...`.
